<hr data-start=3D"97" data-end=3D"100" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"102" data-end=3D"124" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"10=
5" data-end=3D"124">1. Introduction</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"125" data-end=3D"372" class=3D"">Les <strong data-start=3D=
"129" data-end=3D"168">VLANs (Virtual Local Area Networks)</strong> permett=
ent de segmenter un r=C3=A9seau en plusieurs sous-r=C3=A9seaux logiques afi=
n d=E2=80=99am=C3=A9liorer la s=C3=A9curit=C3=A9, la gestion et les perform=
ances. Cependant, par d=C3=A9faut, les VLANs ne peuvent pas communiquer ent=
re eux.</p>
<p data-start=3D"374" data-end=3D"566" class=3D"">Le <strong data-start=3D"=
377" data-end=3D"421">routage entre VLANs (Inter-VLAN Routing)</strong> est=
 la solution qui permet la communication entre des h=C3=B4tes situ=C3=A9s d=
ans des VLANs diff=C3=A9rents. Pour cela, nous avons trois principales m=C3=
=A9thodes :</p>
<ol data-start=3D"567" data-end=3D"802">
<li data-start=3D"567" data-end=3D"639" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"570" data-end=3D"639" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"570=
" data-end=3D"619">Routeur avec des interfaces physiques d=C3=A9di=C3=A9es<=
/strong> (m=C3=A9thode obsol=C3=A8te).</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"640" data-end=3D"726" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"643" data-end=3D"726" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"643=
" data-end=3D"664">Router-on-a-Stick</strong> (utilisation d=E2=80=99une se=
ule interface avec des sous-interfaces).</p>
</li>
