<h4 data-start=3D"1677" data-end=3D"1712" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
1682" data-end=3D"1712">1.1.2. Types de Zones OSPF</strong></h4>
<ul data-start=3D"1713" data-end=3D"2073">
<li data-start=3D"1713" data-end=3D"1837" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1715" data-end=3D"1837" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
715" data-end=3D"1741">Zone Backbone (Area 0)</strong> : La zone centrale d=
u r=C3=A9seau OSPF. Toutes les autres zones doivent =C3=AAtre connect=C3=A9=
es =C3=A0 la zone 0.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1838" data-end=3D"1924" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1840" data-end=3D"1924" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
840" data-end=3D"1858">Zones Standard</strong> : Les zones qui partagent la=
 m=C3=AAme base de donn=C3=A9es de lien d'=C3=A9tat.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1925" data-end=3D"2073" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"1927" data-end=3D"2073" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"1=
927" data-end=3D"1976">Zone Nouvelles de Type Stub et Totally Stubby</stron=
g> : Ces zones sont utilis=C3=A9es pour r=C3=A9duire la quantit=C3=A9 de ro=
uteurs dans les zones non essentielles.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h4 data-start=3D"2075" data-end=3D"2118" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
2080" data-end=3D"2118">1.1.3. Zone Stub et Totally Stubby</strong></h4>
<p data-start=3D"2119" data-end=3D"2359" class=3D"">Une <strong data-start=
=3D"2123" data-end=3D"2136">zone stub</strong> bloque la propagation des in=
formations externes et n'accepte que les routes intra-zone et les routes pa=
r d=C3=A9faut. Une <strong data-start=3D"2254" data-end=3D"2277">zone total=
ly stubby</strong> va encore plus loin en n'acceptant m=C3=AAme pas les inf=
ormations de type <strong data-start=3D"2347" data-end=3D"2358">summary</st=
rong>.</p>
