<h2 data-start=3D"8081" data-end=3D"8107" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
8084" data-end=3D"8107">6. D=C3=A9pannage de STP</strong></h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"8109" data-end=3D"8144" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
8113" data-end=3D"8144">6.1. Commandes de D=C3=A9pannage</strong></h3>
<p data-start=3D"8145" data-end=3D"8219" class=3D"">Pour diagnostiquer des =
probl=C3=A8mes de STP, voici quelques commandes utiles :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"8220" data-end=3D"8653">
<li data-start=3D"8220" data-end=3D"8282" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"8222" data-end=3D"8282" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"8=
222" data-end=3D"8246"><code data-start=3D"8224" data-end=3D"8244">show spa=
nning-tree</code></strong> : Affiche l'=C3=A9tat de l'instance STP.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8283" data-end=3D"8378" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"8285" data-end=3D"8378" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"8=
285" data-end=3D"8324"><code data-start=3D"8287" data-end=3D"8322">show spa=
nning-tree vlan &lt;vlan-id&gt;</code></strong> : Affiche les informations =
STP sp=C3=A9cifiques =C3=A0 un VLAN.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8379" data-end=3D"8489" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"8381" data-end=3D"8489" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"8=
381" data-end=3D"8412"><code data-start=3D"8383" data-end=3D"8410">show spa=
nning-tree detail</code></strong> : Affiche les d=C3=A9tails des ports, y c=
ompris les co=C3=BBts, les chemins et l=E2=80=99=C3=A9tat.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8490" data-end=3D"8554" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"8492" data-end=3D"8554" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"8=
492" data-end=3D"8521"><code data-start=3D"8494" data-end=3D"8519">show spa=
nning-tree root</code></strong> : V=C3=A9rifie l=E2=80=99=C3=A9tat du root =
bridge.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8555" data-end=3D"8653" class=3D"">
<p data-start=3D"8557" data-end=3D"8653" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"8=
557" data-end=3D"8603"><code data-start=3D"8559" data-end=3D"8601">show spa=
nning-tree interface &lt;interface&gt;</code></strong> : Affiche l=E2=80=99=
=C3=A9tat du port pour un port sp=C3=A9cifique.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"8655" data-end=3D"8658" class=3D"">
<h2 data-start=3D"8660" data-end=3D"8680" class=3D""><strong data-start=3D"=
8663" data-end=3D"8680">7. Conclusion</strong></h2>
<p data-start=3D"8682" data-end=3D"9220" class=3D"">Le <strong data-start=
=3D"8685" data-end=3D"8717">Spanning Tree Protocol (STP)</strong> est cruci=
al pour maintenir un r=C3=A9seau local stable et =C3=A9viter les boucles de=
 commutation. Bien que STP soit relativement complexe, sa configuration cor=
recte et sa gestion avanc=C3=A9e permettent d'optimiser la performance et l=
a r=C3=A9silience du r=C3=A9seau. La ma=C3=AEtrise des concepts de STP, ain=
si que des fonctions avanc=C3=A9es telles que <strong data-start=3D"9038" d=
ata-end=3D"9046">RSTP</strong>, <strong data-start=3D"9048" data-end=3D"905=
6">MSTP</strong>, <strong data-start=3D"9058" data-end=3D"9072">Root Guard<=
/strong> et <strong data-start=3D"9076" data-end=3D"9090">BPDU Guard</stron=
g>, est essentielle pour tout ing=C3=A9nieur r=C3=A9seau souhaitant travail=
ler avec des infrastructures de commutation Cisco =C3=A0 grande =C3=A9chell=
e.</p>
<p data-start=3D"9222" data-end=3D"9465" class=3D"">En suivant ce tutoriel,=
